nt":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"The only failure was a missing import: `collections` is allowed but was not imported inside the function. The rule itself was not exercised, so I keep it unchanged and add the import (or, equivalently, count colours with a plain dictionary so the program depends on nothing beyond numpy).\n\n**Recap of the rule.** Each input is a background grid with four equal-size square blocks sitting in the four corners. The colour that appears on the most blocks is the \"mobile\" colour. Every block of that colour slides diagonally toward the centre by exactly its own side length (a 2×2 block moves 2 rows and 2 columns; a 4×4 block moves 4 and 4), the direction being chosen by the corner it sits in: top → down, bottom → up, left → right, right → left. Blocks of the other colours stay put. When shifted blocks overlap (pair 4, the 2×2 fives in a 7×7 grid), the output is simply the union of the shifted blocks.\n\nChecks against the demonstrations: pair 2 — the two 8s move from [0,0,3,3] to [4,4,7,7] and from [0,13,3,16] to [4,9,7,12] while 9 and 1 stay; pair 3 — three 9s move by 3 each, the 2 stays; pair 1 — all four 2s are the same colour, so all four move by 1.\n\n**Test input** (7×30, 2×2 blocks, colours 6/1/1/3): colour 1 is the majority. The top‑right 1 block moves down‑left to rows 2–3, columns 26–27; the bottom‑left 1 block moves up‑right to rows 3–4, columns 2–3. The 6 and 3 blocks remain in place. The unusual width does not matter because shifts are measured from each block's own corner and size.","code":"import collections\n\ndef transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    # majority colour among the corner blocks\n    counts = collections.Counter(o[\"colour\"] for o in objs)\n    mobile = counts.most_common(1)[0][0]\n    movers = [o for o in objs if o[\"colour\"] == mobile]\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    # stage 1: lift the mobile blocks off their corners\n    for o in movers:\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, f\"Colour {mobile} is the majority among the corner blocks; erase those blocks from their corners\")\n\n    # stage 2: stamp each one shifted diagonally inward by its own size\n    for o in movers:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n        dy = h if y0 == 0 else -h\n        dx = w if x0 == 0 else -w\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n            if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W:\n                out[ny, nx] = mobile\n        emit(out, f\"Block at corner ({y0},{x0}) slides {h} rows and {w} columns toward the interior\")\n\n    emit(out, \"All majority-colour blocks moved inward; other blocks unchanged\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every block of that colour slides diagonally toward the centre by exactly its own side length (a 2×2 block moves 2 rows and 2 columns; a 4×4 block moves 4 and 4), the direction being chosen by the corner it sits in: top → down, bottom → up, left → right, right → left."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"When shifted blocks overlap (pair 4, the 2×2 fives in a 7×7 grid), the output is simply the union of the shifted blocks."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block at corner (0,0) slides 1 rows and 1 columns toward the interior"}],"topology":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block at corner (0,0) slides 1 rows and 1 columns toward the interior"},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block at corner (0,15) slides 1 rows and 1 columns toward the interior"},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Block at corner (15,0) slides 1 rows and 1 columns toward the interior"}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rule itself was not exercised, so I keep it unchanged and add the import (or, equivalently, count colours with a plain dictionary so the program depends on nothing beyond numpy)."}],"boolean":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"When shifted blocks overlap (pair 4, the 2×2 fives in a 7×7 grid), the output is simply the union of the shifted blocks."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"When shifted blocks overlap (pair 4, the 2×2 fives in a 7×7 grid), the output is simply the union of the shifted blocks."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"42b329ea1ebac11e727d81627ee6337fca9616e7abcc597fd7d274f65126ede4","task_sha256":"994c667d5a0214ed4dca29e1e7697c3924f19e688e17bfc1cfc2572b6e9b9d4b","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
